# Quick-Tool Radial Menu Implementation TODO

Source proposal: [QuickToolRadialMenu.proposal.md](QuickToolRadialMenu.proposal.md)
Status: Phases 2-5 and the shared-selector investigation are complete at their stated source/integration boundaries (2026-09-23). Phase 5 wires the hold menu, cached entries, local equipment results, localization and lifecycle, with retained traceability, second review and completion audit. Earlier missing evidence records are identified explicitly below. Phase 6 runtime acceptance remains unstarted.

## Goal and scope

Deliver separate reusable radial-menu and quick-tool functionality systems. Holding the key opens the menu; clicking equips the best tool in a category; the center restores previous equipment. Tool-category and virtual-entry positions remain fixed across inventory changes and menu openings. Include a Light source virtual entry using shared existing selection logic. Use client-owned restoration and the game's native inventory flip packets; reconcile interrupted sequences without loss, duplication, or false success.

Exclude favorites, nested menus, external-container tools, reconnect persistence, arbitrary inventory undo, and unrelated light-source feature changes. A behavior-preserving shared selector extraction for the virtual Light source entry is in scope. Full inventory scans on invalidation are allowed; incremental indexing is not required. No per-frame inventory scanning.

## Source evidence and implementation constraints

- Approved rendering: cached combined wedge/center mesh with shader-driven appearance, separate game-rendered icons/text, and CPU hit testing. Performance testing and comparative benchmarks are not required; retain correctness, visual, input, and resource-lifecycle checks without claiming measured speedups.

- Existing integration reference: `VanillaExpanded/src/ModSystems/EquipLightSource.cs` registers client inventory hotkeys and sends packets returned by `Inventory.TryFlipItems`. Its reusable callback accepts an inventory manager and slot rather than requiring a mock player.
- Adjacent API source: `../vsapi/Common/Inventory/InventoryBase.cs` implements `TryFlipItems` by attempting a local slot flip and then producing a packet. The revised implementation deliberately uses this native path one flip at a time. It does not establish server acceptance or atomicity across flips; test interruption and synchronization explicitly.
- API investigation starting points: `IInventory.SlotModified`, `CollectibleObject.GetToolTier(ItemSlot)`, `GetRemainingDurability(ItemStack)`, and `IClientEventAPI.KeyDown/KeyUp`. Verify behavior against the installed assemblies referenced by the project, not just the adjacent source checkout.
- Existing GUI examples live under `VanillaExpanded/src/Gui`; tests use xUnit in `VanillaExpanded.Tests`. Resolve actual build/package wrappers before validation.
- Keep composition roots thin. Proposed domain directories are `VanillaExpanded/src/QuickTools` and `VanillaExpanded/src/RadialMenu`; finalize names after checking repository conventions. Separate candidate selection, restoration, and integration responsibilities without creating extra independent functionality systems or trivial semantic wrappers.
- Add XML documentation to all new classes and methods, explanatory comments for nontrivial logic, and region blocks grouping related methods. Keep planning terminology out of production code and user documentation.
- Delegate all future build/test tool execution to subagents, as required by the supplied repository instructions. Initial plan creation ran no builds or tests; the phase 1 API investigation uses a delegated isolated installed-assembly probe.

## Implementation decisions

The [implementation contract](docs/quick-tool/ImplementationContract.md) records D1-D7 and task-to-document traceability. D1-D7 and phase 1 passed the [second review](docs/quick-tool/SecondReview.md) and [independent completion audit](docs/quick-tool/CompletionAudit.md) on 2026-09-23. Installed evidence is recorded in [InventoryContractEvidence.md](docs/quick-tool/InventoryContractEvidence.md).

✔ D1: Confirm highest tier as the primary criterion, followed by the confirmed preference for lowest remaining absolute durability, then stable inventory/slot order; define unsupported or incomparable metadata handling. Lower-durability usable tools must be preferred within the same tier so tools closest to breaking are used up first.
✔ D2: Define eligible player-owned inventories and supported tool categories, including modded metadata. Exclude opened external containers.
✔ D3: Confirm chained selections preserve the item held before the first selection.
✔ D4: Confirm manual hotbar selection or held-item replacement ends the session.
✔ D5: Choose whether an unusable original return slot permits another eligible empty slot or requires unchanged-inventory failure.
✔ D6: Confirm center disabled without a valid session, or explicitly revise scope for manually equipped tools before implementing that extension.
✔ D7: Define the exact category-to-wedge table, starting angle, direction, and deterministic modded-category extension policy. Disabled wedges for unavailable categories are already required, not an open decision.

## Phase 1: Resolve policy and prove inventory integration contracts

Dependencies: None.
Outcome: A concrete implementation contract and safe inventory movement design are available before production consumers are added.

✔ Resolve D1-D7 in a decision record linked from this plan; reconcile deviations with the approved proposal rather than silently changing scope.
✔ Trace functionality registration, feature enablement/live configuration, player/world lifecycle, localization, and GUI ownership conventions.
✔ Trace key press, held state, release, repeat, pointer capture, click consumption, focus loss, and active-slot change events using current API contracts.
✔ Establish inventory enumeration and subscription lifecycle, including inventories added/replaced after startup and durability/ranking changes without slot replacement.
✔ Establish an item identity contract for duplicate tools, moved stacks, consumed/broken items, and network replacement of client stack objects. Item code alone is insufficient.
✔ Investigate the original server-owned execution and result design; its authority choice was later superseded by the client-owned revision below.
    ✔ Trace client prediction, server validation, accepted/rejected results, and when notifications fire for the original investigation.
    ✔ Describe all intermediate states of A-to-B, B-to-C, and unequip, including restricted/full inventories.
    ✔ Establish the installed direct-assignment primitive for the original design; this is historical evidence and does not establish atomic native multi-flip behavior.
    ✔ Record the original request/result boundary; the client-owned revision removes that adapter in favor of native flip packets.
    ✔ Record the original rejection contract; the revised design permits a safe, explicitly reported partial sequence when a later native flip fails.
✔ Define the module/interface ownership map and focused validation cases, retaining narrow testable inventory/slot seams.

Completion criteria:
✔ Every open decision has a recorded resolution before dependent work starts.
✔ Installed API evidence identifies input, event, identity, authority, and completion hooks; uncertain behavior is explicitly tracked.
✔ The original direct-assignment movement contract was demonstrated by an isolated fixture. That evidence does not validate the revised native sequence; its separate verification is included in Phase 4 below.

### Virtual-entry amendment: prerequisites complete

The earlier completion audit covers the original tool-only contract. The requested virtual-entry addition is specified in Proposal / Virtual Entries and the implementation contract addendum; the incremental investigation is recorded in [VirtualEntryContract.md](docs/quick-tool/VirtualEntryContract.md), with [installed evidence](docs/quick-tool/VirtualEntryEvidence.md) and a passing [independent amendment audit](docs/quick-tool/VirtualEntryCompletionAudit.md). The original audit remains historical evidence for its original scope.

✔ Confirm the side-independent shared light-selector extraction seam and source eligibility, preserving legacy priority and equal-brightness behavior without duplicating logic.
✔ Verify offhand-source validation, persistence, identity, and restoration constraints for virtual selections; update the movement contract and review the amendment before dependent equipment implementation.

## Phase 2: Reusable radial-menu functionality

Dependencies: Phase 1 input and ownership contracts.
Outcome: Generic entries can be displayed and selected without any tool or inventory dependency.

✔ Define menu entries with stable identifiers, labels, icons, enabled state, supplied fixed positions, and a center entry; expose selection/cancellation reporting. Accept tool-category and virtual entries identically without requiring EnumTool in the reusable menu API.
✔ Implement cached mesh rendering and CPU hit detection with a fixed starting angle/direction supplied through the agreed layout contract.
    ✔ Generate and upload one combined mesh for all wedges and the center, with constant per-triangle entry identifiers; draw the background in one call.
    ✔ Cache geometry across frames and reopenings. Rebuild only for geometric layout changes or required resource recreation; use transforms for position and uniform scale.
    ✔ Retain unavailable categories in the mesh. Hover, selection, enabled state, animation, and candidate changes update shader/entry data without rebuilding or re-uploading geometry.
    ✔ Implement shader-driven colors, highlighting, disabled appearance, animation, and edge effects using mesh entry identifiers rather than full-quad per-fragment angular classification.
    ✔ Define arc tessellation tolerance across supported GUI scales and supply antialiasing edge data. Retessellate only when a scale change exceeds the supported tolerance, preserving category positions.
    ✔ Render icons and cached text separately through existing game APIs; maintain a generic icon contract without quick-tool policy in the radial-menu system.
    ✔ Match CPU hit testing to rendering coordinates, radii, angular layout, direction, and separator/boundary behavior; send the hovered identifier to the shader without GPU readback.
    ✔ Own shader reload, mesh disposal/recreation, and render-state restoration within the radial-menu system; use radial-progress rendering conventions where applicable.
✔ Preserve supplied wedge geometry when entry content or availability changes. Disabled entries remain visible and cannot be selected.
✔ Implement pointer highlighting, center selection, and open/close lifetime with input capture and restoration.
✔ Provide the caller interaction hooks needed for hold-to-open, release-to-cancel, click-to-commit, and suppression of repeated opening until release.
✔ Consume the selection click and release input ownership on cancellation, focus loss, feature disablement, and world exit.
✔ Verify boundary/center hit detection, disabled entries, exactly-once selection, cancellation without selection, and cleanup; demonstrate generic non-tool entries in focused verification rather than shipping a separate sample feature.

Completion criteria:
✔ Menu logic has no tool ranking, inventory movement, or restoration dependency.
✔ Entry updates preserve geometry and selection identity; every exit path restores input ownership.
✔ Focused correctness checks demonstrate geometry reuse for state/candidate changes and reopenings, permitted rebuilds for geometry changes, and consistent center/wedge boundary selection. No timing measurements or comparative benchmarks are required.
✔ Focused automated evidence covers logic; actual camera/click/focus behavior remains an explicit live-runtime check in Phase 6.

Implementation retained in commit `7d66d77`. The previously linked review files are absent; see [current dependency evidence and provenance](docs/quick-tool/Phase5Traceability.md#prior-evidence-references).

## Phase 3: Deterministic category layout and candidate cache

Dependencies: Phase 1 D1, D2, D7 and inventory events, plus virtual-entry selector prerequisites; Phase 2 entry contract.
Outcome: Quick-tool entries reflect current best candidates while retaining stable positions.

✔ Implement the canonical category-to-wedge mapping owned by quick-tools and supplied to the menu.
    ✔ Build identical layouts for the same supported set/configuration regardless of registration or inventory enumeration order.
    ✔ Retain disabled wedges for missing tools; never compact, rotate, or resize because of inventory changes.
    ✔ Apply the modded-category policy using stable category identifiers. Discovering an item cannot insert a wedge dynamically.
    ✔ Permit structural rebuild only for supported-category-set or explicit configuration changes, deferred until the menu is closed.
✔ Introduce a small stable entry/provider contract for tool categories and virtual selections, with cached real-slot candidates; keep semantic selection outside radial-menu rendering.
✔ Add `virtual:light-source` at fixed index 33 after tool indices 0-32 (34 outer entries total); retain its disabled wedge when absent. This explicit supported-set amendment changes the planned layout once; inventory changes never change positions.
✔ Extract/reuse the existing light-source selector as one shared side-independent owner consumed by EquipLightSource and the virtual provider. Preserve offhand, active-hand, brightest-hotbar, brightest-backpack priority, positive LightHsv[2] recognition, and stable equal-brightness ties; do not apply tool-tier/durability ranking to this provider.
✔ Include offhand-source observation and active-hotbar changes in virtual candidate invalidation, with no per-frame inventory scan; verify current selector inputs again on selection.
✔ Add focused selection parity coverage for lanterns/torches/oil lamps or representative light collectibles, no light, held/offhand preference, equal brightness, brighter backpack versus dimmer hotbar, and legacy hotkey behavior after extraction.
✔ Implement eligible inventory discovery, authoritative category classification, usability filtering, ranking, and deterministic tie-breaking.
✔ Cache the winning candidate per category with rendering data and a resolvable location hint, not an assumed reservation.
✔ Build the initial cache; subscribe to inventory/ranking changes and coalesce notifications from an operation into one refresh after completion.
✔ Refresh pending invalidation before opening; update visible candidate content/enabled state without moving wedges.
✔ Revalidate cached candidate identity, availability, and destination before any equipment operation. Never trust a stale slot reference.
✔ Unsubscribe from replaced inventories and on disable/world exit; discard stale references and rebuild for the current player when enabled.
✔ Verify best-candidate changes, deterministic ties, unsupported metadata, empty inventories, excluded containers, durability changes, notification batching, stale entries, and inventory replacement. Include a regression case proving that a usable tool with lower remaining absolute durability beats a higher-durability tool of the same tier, with deterministic ties and unusable/broken tools excluded.
✔ Verify identical wedge positions across reopenings, acquisition/removal, inventory reorder, ranking changes, and shuffled category registration.

Completion criteria:
✔ Chosen ranking and eligibility policies are covered by focused tests.
✔ Cache invalidation covers every ranking input without a per-frame inventory scan.
✔ Layout stability is demonstrated independently from candidate-selection determinism.

During selector extraction, strengthen existing brightest-source tests to assert the exact winning slot/item, including a brightest candidate at the end, and remove stale test commentary. The independent amendment audit identified these as part of the planned parity coverage.

Implementation retained in commit `2084c29`. The previously linked review files are absent; see [current dependency evidence and provenance](docs/quick-tool/Phase5Traceability.md#prior-evidence-references).

## Phase 4: Client-owned equipment and restoration

Dependencies: revised client native-movement contract and D3-D6, including the virtual offhand-source amendment; Phase 3 candidate resolution.
Outcome: Client-owned quick-tool history resolves tracked ItemStack references at action time and uses the game's supported inventory flips. Implementation and focused local verification satisfy this revised contract.

✔ Reduce transient session state to original ItemStack reference or empty-hand state, current quick-tool ItemStack reference, original active hotbar position, and preferred home address. Retain quantity/entry validation metadata; remove the expected displaced-item slot. The home address is only a return destination. Keep server inventory authority in the native flip handler.
✔ Resolve each tracked ItemStack reference exactly once in the current eligible inventory before switch/restore and before presenting restoration availability on menu open. Do not trust cached source slots or match by item ID/serialized contents. Recheck resolved slots before each flip; missing or ambiguous references invalidate the session without movement.
✔ Equip a selected tool or virtual-entry candidate through `TryFlipItems` and send exactly the returned native packet. Already-held selections are no-ops and create no artificial session.
✔ Rerun the known entry provider client-side immediately before selection; reject unknown or stale entries. Use the same native-flip route for Light, including the physical offhand. Verify tool-to-light-to-tool chains, restoring A, aliases resolving to one stack, and incompatible offhand destinations.
✔ Implement chained selection according to D3 with sequential flips: return B home, then equip C while carrying original A forward. Recheck each next flip and stop on interruption; never claim the series is atomic.
✔ Implement center restoration, including initially empty hands and D5 occupied-home fallback. Preserve unrelated items and never drop/delete to make room. If no valid route exists before the first flip, reject unchanged.
✔ Simplify QuickToolClientOperations to lifecycle, current player context, and synchronous reentrancy handling. Remove equipment slot-change subscriptions, deferred reconciliation flags, and callback/response/timeout gates. Candidate-cache inventory subscriptions remain owned by the cache.
✔ Validate the actual local result immediately after each flip and update session state after the sequence. Permit the next action immediately with fresh lookup. Do not classify later inventory updates as request acceptance/rejection. Preserve existing interruption behavior without speculative rollback.
✔ Handle session lifetime explicitly under action-time validation.
    ✔ Unrelated inventory changes affect the candidate cache without driving session reconciliation.
    ✔ Apply D4: clear immediately on manual active-hotbar change; detect held-stack replacement or movement out of that hand at menu-open/action validation.
    ✔ Find moved original stacks by reference at their current eligible location. Validate the preferred home independently and apply D5 when unavailable.
    ✔ Revalidate consumed, removed, broken, split/merged, replaced, or ambiguously duplicated references at action time. Native object replacement ends restoration even if contents match; do not infer why it occurred.
    ✔ Clear transient state on world exit, player replacement, and feature disablement.
✔ Verify exact native packet order, item conservation, final locations, original-item continuity, and session state for A-to-B-to-unequip and A-to-B-to-C-to-unequip after this revision.
✔ Cover empty hand, already-held tool, aliases, duplicates, restricted slots, full inventory, changed home, moved original/current stacks, consumed/broken/replaced stacks, failed first flip, and interrupted later flip. Include movement without a slot callback, successive actions without server updates or ticks, callback-independent session state, and later object replacement discovered by the next lookup. Preserve candidate-cache event coverage.
✔ Update traceability, perform a second review, and repeat the independent completion audit against the revised proposal and linked contracts before completing reopened tasks.

Completion criteria:
✔ Successful restoration matches the approved examples using current reference lookup; preflight rejection leaves inventory unchanged, and an interrupted sequence reports its actual partial state.
✔ Tests demonstrate no loss, duplication, unintended identical-item substitution, or false success under the revised client/native-flip boundary. Equipment operations have no pending server-result or slot-callback state.
✔ Local fixture evidence is kept distinct from native multiplayer acceptance still required in Phase 6. Phase 4 does not claim live server acceptance or synchronization proof.

Implementation retained in commit `e0389e6`. The previously linked review files are absent; see [current dependency evidence and provenance](docs/quick-tool/Phase5Traceability.md#prior-evidence-references).

## Phase 5: Quick-tool integration and user feedback

Dependencies: Phases 2-4.
Outcome: The feature is usable through its configured keybind with reusable menu and domain logic kept separate.

✔ Extend the existing QuickToolSystem and QuickToolClientOperations from Phase 4 with menu/cache/keybind integration. Reuse their equipment and lifecycle ownership; do not register a second equipment owner. Wire feature enablement and cleanup using existing conventions.
✔ Register and localize the keybind; hold opens, release without click cancels, click selects once and closes, and continued hold cannot reopen until release/repress.
✔ Supply cached entries with the resolved items' game-localized names (including Light), leaving unavailable wedges unlabeled, and canonical geometry to the radial-menu system; route selected identifiers through current candidate validation and equipment execution.
✔ Keep the center labeled Unequip and explain restoration where applicable. Call QuickToolClientOperations.ValidateRestoration once when preparing menu-open availability under D6; HasSession alone does not establish a valid reference or return route. Use Restore on click for fresh validation, and handle changes since opening. Never scan inventory every render frame.
✔ Localize and display the Light source entry using its resolved item's icon and availability; keep provider-specific policy out of radial-menu code.
✔ Handle the return value of Select/Restore for localized rejected, interrupted, or session-invalidated feedback, including calls that return before LocalResult fires. Avoid duplicate feedback if also subscribing to LocalResult. LocallyApplied describes the local operation, not server confirmation. Guard synchronous reentrancy and prevent gameplay click-through; do not add pending-server-result UI.
✔ Separate menu lifetime from equipment lifetime. Ordinary menu close, release/cancel, and focus loss release input and UI resources while preserving an intact equipment session across openings. Manual active-hotbar change, feature disablement, player/world replacement, and disposal clear the session; menu-open/action validation handles invalid stack references. Candidate-cache subscriptions follow feature/player lifetime.
✔ Add integration verification for input-to-selection-to-local result, stale open-menu candidates, changes between menu-open validation and click, disabled center/categories, immediate reopen-and-select without callbacks/ticks, and synchronous reentrancy. Verify menu close/cancel/focus loss preserve restoration, while equipment lifecycle events clear it.
✔ Review source layout, dependency direction, documentation comments, region organization, and absence of unnecessary wrapper methods.

Completion criteria:
✔ Both functionality systems are independently owned; radial-menu code has no quick-tool dependency.
✔ End-to-end integration satisfies the approved interaction sequence and preserves existing equip-light behavior.

Completion evidence: [traceability](docs/quick-tool/Phase5Traceability.md), [second review](docs/quick-tool/Phase5SecondReview.md), and [independent completion audit](docs/quick-tool/Phase5CompletionAudit.md). Production build passed; 136 focused tests passed. Live acceptance remains in Phase 6.

## Phase 6: Runtime acceptance and delivery evidence

Dependencies: Phase 5.
Outcome: Evidence supports the feature's behavior in the actual game and supported multiplayer setup.

☐ Delegate focused tests and required repository build/check commands to subagents; record commands, versions, results, and remaining failures. Broaden only where required or justified by integration changes.
☐ Use the repository's package workflow if required for loading the mod; verify menu/localization assets are included. Do not publish or deploy as part of this plan.
☐ Verify live interaction: hold, mouse direction, center/wedge click, cancel, continued hold, camera suppression, no world click-through, focus loss, and cleanup.
☐ Verify smooth arcs and separators, highlight/disabled appearance, icon/text composition, input alignment across supported GUI scales, and shader reload/resource cleanup. These are visual and correctness checks, not performance tests.
☐ Verify fixed category locations through repeated openings, inventory rearrangement, acquisition/removal, candidate upgrades, and missing-tool disabled wedges.
☐ Verify A-to-B-to-unequip, empty-hand restoration, and A-to-B-to-C-to-unequip with actual slot contents before/after, including mixed tool/light selections and supported offhand-source restoration.
☐ Verify restoration across separate menu openings, ordinary close/cancel/focus loss, and selections separated by normal gameplay. Move the original ItemStack to another eligible slot before restoring; verify current-location lookup and unrelated-item preservation. Verify reference loss between menu open and click produces local unavailable feedback without substitution.
☐ Verify conflict cases: manual hotbar change, moved/removed/broken items, unavailable return slots, no valid fallback, and feature/world lifecycle changes.
☐ Verify native multiplayer accepted updates and rejected-request corrections, immediate successive local actions, and subsequent reference lookup after synchronization. Record whether accepted updates replace tracked ItemStack objects and how often this ends restoration. Do not infer a per-request result from slot changes. Document unexecuted scenarios; unit tests do not establish live acceptance.
☐ Document user controls, stable category locations, ranking, restoration behavior, fallback, and limitations using existing project documentation conventions.
☐ Perform a second completion review against the proposal and record evidence by source, unit, integration, package, and live-runtime boundary. Remove disposable scaffolding; retain meaningful regression coverage.

Completion criteria:
☐ Required checks and runtime scenarios have recorded evidence; unverified work remains unchecked.
☐ No temporary production bypasses or unsupported compatibility paths remain.

## Proposal coverage and final acceptance

- Functional requirements and system responsibilities: Phases 1-5.
- Interaction and deterministic positions: Phases 2, 3, 5, 6.
- Approved cached-mesh rendering, shader effects, and rendering lifecycle: Phases 2 and 6; no performance-testing gate.
- Candidate discovery, ranking, cache and invalidation: Phases 1, 3, 6.
- Virtual Light source entry, shared selector parity, fixed placement, client provider revalidation, and offhand-source restoration: phase 1 amendment and Phases 2-6.
- Equipment, restoration, external inventory changes: Phases 1, 4-6.
- Native inventory authority, failure handling, and client operation gating: Phases 1, 4-6.
- Decisions to confirm: D1-D7, resolved before dependent implementation.
- Scope boundaries: Goal and scope; no unrelated refactor or speculative extensions.

☐ Every approved proposal requirement is implemented and evidenced, or a change/deferment is explicitly agreed and documented; unresolved requirements are not reported as complete.
☐ Required code, focused tests, integration checks, runtime evidence, user documentation, and applicable package verification are complete.
☐ Deterministic category geometry, safe inventory restoration, reusable menu ownership, and lifecycle cleanup each pass their acceptance checks.
☐ Final report clearly distinguishes implemented behavior, validated behavior, and remaining limitations.
